---
/* THE LIMIT OF WHAT WE ARE CLAIMING.
 *
 * This site tells people their bag will or will not be refused. Some of them
 * will act on that — buy a bag, skip paying for a cabin allowance, turn up at
 * a gate confident. So the honest terms are mostly about the boundary of the
 * claim, and that boundary is real rather than defensive boilerplate:
 *
 *   - we read the airline's published policy, we do not observe gates;
 *   - airlines change limits without notice and enforce unevenly, which is
 *     why the engine has a strictness axis at all;
 *   - most dimensions are the manufacturer's, not ours — we have measured
 *     zero bags, and the release gate exists precisely because we know the
 *     difference matters.
 *
 * The disclaimer here is therefore an extension of /methodology rather than a
 * legal fig leaf, and it should read like one. If it ever starts sounding like
 * a generator wrote it, that is the signal it has stopped being true.
 *
 * NOT WRITTEN BY A LAWYER, said at the foot. Frank: "They can be imperfect for
 * the moment but please stub them out."
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const UPDATED = 'September 2026';
---
<Base
  title="Terms — Sizer"
  description="What Sizer is claiming when it tells you a bag fits, and what it is not."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-page px-6">

    <section class="pt-12 pb-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Terms of use</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-lg text-ink-2">
        The short version: this is information, carefully sourced, and it is
        not a guarantee about what will happen at a gate.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">Last updated {UPDATED}.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What a verdict means</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        When we say a bag fits, we mean its published outside dimensions fall
        within the limit that airline publishes for that fare, as we read it on
        the date shown beside the source.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        We do not observe gates. We have not tested your bag against that
        airline's sizer. Airlines change their limits without notice, run
        different rules on different aircraft and routes, and enforce the same
        published policy very unevenly — which is why the tool asks how much
        risk you want to take rather than pretending there is one answer.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        A verdict is our best reading of the published rule. The gate agent is
        the one who decides.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Airline limits are read from the airline's own baggage pages. Bag
        dimensions are, in almost every case, the manufacturer's published
        figures — we have measured no bags ourselves, and we say so on every
        page rather than implying otherwise.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Manufacturers are not consistent about whether wheels and handles are
        included, and some publish only the packing space inside. Where a
        figure does not meet our standard, the bag is shown but never put
        forward as a recommendation.
        <a href="/methodology/" class="text-blue hover:text-blue-hover hover:underline underline-offset-2">How we work it out</a>
        sets out the whole ladder.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Your decisions are yours</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Use this site as one input, not as the only one — and check the
        airline's own page before you fly, especially if you are relying on a
        tight margin. We are not liable for gate fees, refused bags, missed
        flights or purchases made on the strength of what you read here.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        The site is provided as it is, without warranty. We try hard to be
        accurate and we will correct errors when we find them, but we cannot
        promise the site is complete, current or uninterrupted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Content and trade marks</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Airline and manufacturer names, logos and product names belong to their
        owners and are used here to identify what we are describing. No
        affiliation with or endorsement by any of them is claimed or implied.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        The writing, design and analysis on this site are ours. Quoting us with
        a link is welcome; wholesale republication is not.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Changes</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        These terms may change as the site grows. The date at the top says when
        they last did.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8 text-sm text-muted">
      This is an early draft written by the site's author, not by a lawyer, and
      it is not legal advice.
    </p>

  </div>
</Base>
